Manuel wrote:

> BTW, I must say that some of the most pleasant games were those
> where I found out things by myself. For example, once I knew
> what Sokoban was about, I decided to solve it unspoiled; I messed
> it up a few times, and then managed to solve it (or most of it)
> unspoiled. That took a lot of thinking, but I enjoyed it.

That's not exactly what I would call spoiling.  I always solve Sokoban 
by figuring it out myself (or by memory), but this has very little to do 
with trying to remain unspoiled.  Sokoban is just a game in the game, 
where you don't need any additional knowledge to solve it; thinking is 
sufficient, all the requisites are at hand, once you enter the level. 
Finding out about the invocation ritual or the necessity of taking the 
silver bell from the quest along or the importance of magic cancellation 
is not something you will achieve by pure thinking.  You have to acquire 
knowledge, not just to think; this may be achieved in some cases by 
making key experiences in the game, in most cases by reading about it, 
i.e. by getting spoiled.
